  bx('pk', "<b>Teacher's board:</b> INC = <b>1st all India ORG</b> but <b>not all Indian</b> (Founder = A.O. Hume). Rule: President not from the session's region: Bonnerjee (Bengali) at Bombay; Yule 1888 (Agency House in Calcutta) at Allahabad. Hume = <b>LIBERAL</b> (representative governance), Retd ICS, belief in White Man's Burden; wanted a strong opposition so Government of India is accountable + responsive. Earlier bodies were only attempts at an all-India ORG."),
  bx('bl', "<b>Handout figure: INC's four objectives:</b> close ties among workers; dissolve race, creed, province barriers; record conclusions; a programme for next year."),
  sec("PRESIDENTS AND FIRSTS " + P('1998') + P('2000') + P('2015'), 'r'),
  tb(["Figure", "Distinction", "Session"], ["A.O. Hume", "Founder + General Secretary: <b>NEVER President</b>", "none"], ["<b>W.C. Bonnerjee 1885</b>", "1st President", "Bombay 1885"], ["<b>Dadabhai Naoroji 1886</b>", "2nd session", "Calcutta 1886"], ["<b>Badruddin Tyabji 1887</b>", "<b>1st Muslim</b> President", "Madras 1887"], ["<b>George Yule 1888</b>", "<b>1st non-Indian</b> President", "Allahabad 1888"], ["<b>William Wedderburn 1889</b>", "<b>2nd non-Indian</b> (1889 and 1910)", ""], ["<b>Alfred Webb 1894</b>", "<b>3rd non-Indian</b>", "Madras 1894"], ["<b>Annie Besant 1917</b>", "<b>1st woman</b>", "Calcutta 1917"], ["<b>C.R. Das 1922</b>", "Presided <b>from prison</b>", "Gaya 1922"], ["<b>Sarojini Naidu 1925</b>", "<b>2nd woman</b> (1st Indian woman)", "Kanpur 1925"]),
  bx('pk', "<b>WEMC</b> (Western Educated Middle Class) formed the early INC. <b>Teacher's exam-marked firsts:</b> Bonnerjee, Naoroji 1886, Tyabji, Yule, Besant, Naidu. Wedderburn = Retd ICS, Hume's follower. Prelims 2000: (b) 2 and 4 (Das from prison Gaya 1922; Webb 1894; St 1 false: Besant first; St 3 false: Hume never President, first Britisher = Yule 1888). Prelims 2015: (b) Only 2 (Besant first, Naidu second; Tyabji Madras 1887)."),
  bx('gr', "<b>First and second sessions (Prelims 2004, 2008):</b> first session <b>Bombay</b> (not Calcutta), 2nd <b>Calcutta 1886 under Naoroji</b>; INC and Muslim League both at <b>Lucknow 1916</b> = Lucknow Pact; answer (c) 2 and 3. Prelims 2008: (b) Bombay; Calcutta classic trap."),
  bx('pk', "<b>Curzon's remark (Prelims 1998):</b> &quot;Congress is tottering to its fall... assist it to a peaceful demise&quot; = <b>Curzon 99-05</b> (Viceroy 1899-1905), (b). Dufferin 84-88 = founding era; Lytton 76-80 left 1880, five years before INC."),
  bx('pk', "<b>Prelims 1998 pairs:</b> (d) 1, 2 and 3. 1 Theodore Beck = MAO College Aligarh (1st Principal; + Sir S.A. Khan, British funded AMU); 2 Ilbert Bill 1883-84 = Ripon 80-84; 3 Pherozeshah Mehta = INC founder-member; <b>4 wrong: Tyabji = INC (1st Muslim President Madras 1887), died Aug 1906, League Dec 1906 Dacca</b>."),
  sec("SAFETY VALVE THEORY: THE DEBATE", 'g'),
  t("<b>What it says</b>", "Hume founded INC as a deliberate British strategy: educated elite vent grievances, <b>prevent a violent mass uprising</b>", "Origin: <b>Wedderburn's 1913 biography of Hume</b>; <b>1878</b> Hume found <b>7 volumes of secret reports</b> by religious gurus (lower-class conspiracy); met <b>Dufferin 84-88</b> (Viceroy 1884-88); also backed by <b>R.P. Dutt</b>", "Teacher: <b>whistle, lid, cooker</b> (Indians = steam, burner = British oppression, INC = whistle); exam verdict: <b>never in exam, always on YouTube</b>", w=24),
  bx('ye', "<b>Date puzzle (source):</b> handout puts the volumes in 1878 and the Dufferin meeting after, though Dufferin 84-88 became Viceroy only 1884; the PYQ answer places the meeting in 1885. Use: reports 1878, Dufferin 84-88 term 1884-88, INC Dec 1885."),
  t("<b>Why it is wrong: three flaws</b>", "<b>(i) Archive:</b> 7 volumes <b>never found</b>; only source Wedderburn, close friend, nearly <b>four decades</b> later", "<b>(ii) Babus:</b> Dufferin 84-88 hated the Moderates: <b>&quot;microscopic minority of Babus working for self-interest&quot;</b>", "<b>(iii) Home Rule:</b> quietly asked Governor of Bombay to be alert; feared an <b>Irish Home Rule League</b> (Charles Parnell, 1870s-80s; Irish self-governance in Empire): a warning, not a compliment", "Whole theory = single unverifiable source", w=24),
  sec("WOULD THE INC HAVE FORMED WITHOUT HUME?", 'b'),
  bx('pk', "<b>Yes (the more important point):</b> activity rose steadily before 1885; regional associations' demands unmet; logical next step = all-India organisation."),
  tb(["Pre-1885 campaign", "Detail"], ["Lex Loci Act", "Protests against 1850 Act"], ["Income Tax 1868", "Nationwide protests"], ["Civil Services 1877-80", "Campaign (1877) for Indianisation"], ["Afghan War + VPA 1878", "Campaigns vs Afghan War (1878-80) and VPA"], ["Ilbert Bill 1883-84", "Active support"], ["Volunteer Corps", "Demand to join Europeans-only units"], ["Appeal to British voters 1885", "Vote for pro-Indian candidates; led by <b>Dadabhai Naoroji</b>; Hume also published a direct appeal"]),
  t("<b>Indian Association 1876</b>", "<b>Surendranath Banerjea</b>, editor of <b>Bengalee</b> (NPA = Native Press Association President 1877); permanent body = <b>regional political party</b> (Bengal)", "Launched <b>Indian National Conference 1883</b> (periodic, delegates from across British India) = attempt at all-India ORG", "Conference met <b>1883 and 1885</b>, <b>merged into INC 1886</b>; Indian Association continued as regional body", w=22),
  tb(["All-India effort", "Year and what it did"], ["<b>British India Association</b>", "1851: tried to open branches in Bombay + Madras for joint London petition (Charter renewal 1853)"], ["<b>Native Press Association</b>", "1877: journalists at Imperial Darbar, all-India; <b>SNB President</b>, annual meetings; <b>not a political party</b> (teacher)"], ["<b>National Conference</b>", "1883, Indian Association, Calcutta; another set for 1885; near-identical tracks to INC so 1886 merger natural"], ["<b>Theosophical Society</b>", "1884: delegates gathered at annual convention, Madras"], ["<b>National Fund</b>", "1885: for political campaigns in India + London (appeal to British voters)"], ["Verdict", "Machinery already being built; 1885 = natural culmination, not British invention"]),
